# Solve many IK queries at once on the GPU.
#
# This is the query shape a GPU is actually good at, and the one this robot has: choosing where to
# cut a grape cluster means proposing dozens of candidate poses around it and asking which are
# reachable. One-at-a-time IK pays its fixed cost per query; here it is paid once for the batch.
#
# On SUCCESS, and why it is not simply cuRobo's own success flag: cuRobo requires its optimiser to
# have CONVERGED, which is stricter than "the answer is inside tolerance" -- measured on this robot,
# a solution 0.67 mm and 0.0016 rad from the goal is reported as a failure. So the errors are
# returned per query and `solved` is computed here against the tolerances below. A caller that wants
# cuRobo's own opinion can tighten the tolerances instead.
string planning_group
# Which link the poses describe. Empty uses the service's configured tool frame. The offset from
# this link to the frame the solver actually solves for is composed away before solving, exactly as
# the CPU Behaviors do -- get it wrong and every answer is off by the tool length.
string target_link
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped[] poses
# Optional starting point. Empty uses the robot's current state.
sensor_msgs/JointState seed_state
# 0 uses the service's configured defaults.
float64 position_tolerance       # metres
float64 orientation_tolerance    # radians
# Reject solutions that collide with the planning scene or the robot itself.
bool collision_free
---
bool success                     # the batch ran; per-query outcomes are in `solved`
string message
# One entry per requested pose, in the same order. An unsolved entry still carries the closest
# solution found, so a caller can see how near it got rather than only that it failed.
sensor_msgs/JointState[] solutions
bool[] solved
float64[] position_error         # metres
float64[] orientation_error      # radians
float64 solve_time               # seconds, for the whole batch
